\section{Build a core collection by forward selection}

\cde{st.detect.core} builds a core collection from one phased population.
Here the candidates are the same 83 base lines used for enrichment, and
we reuse the same five-marker block borders. Forward selection starts
with an empty core and adds the line contributing the largest number of
previously unrepresented qualifying haplotypes at each step.

Allow all base lines as an upper limit; selection stops earlier if no
remaining line provides a positive gain.

<<core-select,eval=FALSE>>=
core <- st.detect.core(
    pool               = "base.phased",
    n.max              = length(base.ids),
    blocks             = blocks,
    exclude.compatible = TRUE,
    method             = "forward"
)
@
The returned \cde{genotypes} list contains the nested selected line sets.
The \cde{segments} vector reports their cumulative numbers of distinct
complete haplotypes, counted separately for each block. Shared sequences
count once within a block. The block borders remain fixed.

Tabulate the number of included lines and represented haplotypes, showing
the first three steps and the final core size.

<<core-summary,eval=FALSE>>=
core.curve <- data.frame(
    Lines = lengths(core$genotypes), # Each entry is the whole core
    Haplotypes = core$segments      # Cumulative, not marginal gains
)
print(rbind(head(core.curve, 3), tail(core.curve, 1)),
      row.names = FALSE)
@
\begin{Soutput}
 Lines Haplotypes
     1        169
     2        321
     3        452
    80       2218
\end{Soutput}

The plot shows the percentage of complete block-specific haplotypes
represented by each core, relative to all haplotypes in the full base
population. Horizontal guides mark 80\% and 90\% coverage; vertical guides
identify the smallest cores reaching those levels: 30 and 43 lines,
respectively. Steps reflect the inclusion of whole lines.

