\section{Literature review and methodological comparison}

Selection of individuals that collectively represent genetic variation has
long been a central problem in the conservation and utilization of genetic
resources. Allelic richness provides a direct criterion when the objective
is to retain distinct variants rather than reproduce their population
frequencies. Schoen and Brown (1993) investigated sampling strategies that
maximize the number of alleles represented in collections of fixed size.
Their work established the value of marker information for allocating
limited sampling resources. Gouesnard et al. (2001) subsequently implemented
the maximization of allelic or phenotypic richness in MSTRAT. These studies
provide the conceptual basis for selecting complementary individuals:
accessions carrying variants already represented contribute less additional
richness than accessions carrying previously unrepresented variants.

Retention of a predefined collection during subsequent expansion also has
a precedent in plant genetic resources. Le Cunff et al. (2008) used MSTRAT
to construct nested grapevine core collections from SSR genotypes. A core
of 48 varieties represented the alleles retained after excluding the rarest
variants. This collection was then retained as the starting set for an
extension to 92 varieties that captured all 326 SSR alleles in the original
collection. The additional accessions therefore supplied diversity absent
from an existing core. Although this application concerned individual SSR
loci and sought complete coverage, it demonstrates that augmentation of a
fixed collection according to missing alleles is an established strategy.

Haplotype-based selection has been developed extensively for allocating
sequencing resources. Druet et al. (2014) investigated the selection of
individuals for whole-genome sequencing and its consequences for genotype
imputation and genomic prediction. Their AHAP approaches used haplotypes
inferred from SNP-panel information to increase the diversity represented
by the sequenced individuals. The AHAP* criterion assigned equal weight
to rare and common haplotypes. This provides a close precedent for an
objective based on the number of distinct haplotypes rather than solely
on pedigree relationships or the frequency of common variants. The target
was a sequencing reference set, however, rather than the incremental
contribution of donors to a specified breeding population.

Bickhart et al. (2016) addressed redundancy in sequencing panels using
nonoverlapping marker segments in Holstein cattle. Their iterative AHAP2
procedure selected an individual, removed its covered haplotypes from
further consideration, and recalculated the scores of the remaining
candidates. Their inverse-weight selection procedure applied the same
iterative principle while favouring lower-frequency haplotypes. The main
selection analyses considered homozygous haplotypes above a frequency
threshold and sought the smallest set of animals covering those targets.
The repeated removal of covered variants is directly relevant to forward
selection: the contribution of each subsequent candidate is conditional
on the individuals already chosen.

Gonen et al. (2017) implemented a related strategy in AlphaSeqOpt. Haplotypes
of a specified number of markers were defined from phased data, their
frequencies were calculated, and focal individuals were selected
successively. After each selection, the chosen individual's haplotypes
were masked in the remaining population. Repetition produced a specified
number of focal individuals. The procedure favoured frequent haplotypes
because sequencing them facilitates imputation in many other individuals.
A further component allocated sequencing resources among focal individuals
and their relatives. This application combines marker-window haplotypes,
a sample-size constraint, and conditional selection, but its frequency and
sequencing objectives differ from an unweighted count of additional variants.

Joint optimization of haplotype representation has also been formulated
using integer programming. Cheng et al. (2021) represented haplotype
carriage by a binary incidence matrix and identified a minimum set of
animals containing all target haplotypes. Their complete-coverage problem
was solved exactly, whereas a further formulation for limited sequencing
resources included frequency-based scores and restrictions on redundancy.
These formulations show how collective haplotype representation can be
addressed by combinatorial optimization. The distinction between minimizing
the number of individuals required for complete coverage and maximizing
coverage for a fixed number of individuals remains important, as these
are different optimization problems.

In crop improvement, haplotypes also provide a basis for identifying useful
variation and choosing complementary donors. Brinton et al. (2020)
developed a haplotype-led framework for wheat breeding that connected
characterization of genomic diversity with its targeted exploitation.
Sanchez et al. (2025) investigated the incorporation of genetic resources
into elite flint maize and used a haplotype criterion based on estimated
breeding values. Their forward donor-selection procedure accounted for
complementarity with recipients and previously introduced donors. These
studies connect haplotype information with breeding decisions, although
the value of a haplotype for a trait and its contribution to variant
richness are distinct quantities. A previously absent variant need not
have a favourable agronomic effect.

The enrichment approach presented here applies these principles to a
fixed base population and a separate pool of candidate donors. Both
populations are phased before selection and evaluated using common block
borders. The borders can be generated from haplotyping options applied to
the base population or supplied explicitly, for example after an LD-based
analysis of that population. For block $b$, let $H_{B,b}$ denote the set
of haplotypes represented in the base population and $H_{d,b}$ the set
carried by donor $d$. For a selected donor set $S$, additional haplotype
richness is
\[
 E(S)=\sum_b\left|
       \left(\bigcup_{d\in S}H_{d,b}\right)
       \setminus H_{B,b}\right|,
 \qquad |S|=n.
\]
Both homologues contribute when a donor is heterozygous. Each distinct
additional haplotype is counted once within its block, irrespective of the
number of selected donors carrying it. An increase of one therefore means
one additional block-specific variant, not one additional variant in every
block. The count must also be distinguished from the number of blocks that
gain at least one variant: a single block can contribute several additions.

This objective is a haplotype-level form of allelic-richness maximization.
A haplotype block can be regarded as a multiallelic locus whose alleles are
phased marker sequences. Keeping the base population fixed makes selection
conditional on diversity already available, as in the extension of nested
core collections. Relative to sequencing-panel applications, the relevant
variants are those absent from the base, and each eligible addition has
the same weight. Relative to trait-oriented donor selection, no estimated
marker or haplotype effects enter the criterion. Separate donor pools can
thus be compared for their potential contribution of additional diversity,
provided that the marker coding and block definitions are comparable.

Incomplete haplotypes require an explicit distinction between observed
absence and uncertain absence. Complete donor haplotypes can be compared
with complete base haplotypes directly. When exclusion of compatible
variants is requested, a donor haplotype that matches the observed markers
of an incomplete base homologue is not credited as an unambiguous addition
in the selection score. This conservative rule acknowledges that the
variant may already be present but partially unobserved. Its eligibility
assessment is fixed relative to the original base population. Phasing and
imputation quality nevertheless affect the inferred sequences, and the
resulting enrichment is conditional on those inferred data and on the
chosen block resolution.

With fixed blocks and eligibility rules, each donor supplies a fixed set
of eligible block-specific variants absent from the base. Selection then
maximizes the size of the union of these sets. The objective is monotone
and submodular: adding a donor cannot reduce coverage, and its marginal
contribution cannot increase as more donors are selected. Best-subset
search determines a globally optimal set when completed, whereas forward
selection repeatedly adds the donor with the greatest remaining marginal
contribution. The latter need not attain the optimum. Applying the result
of Nemhauser et al. (1978), its coverage satisfies
\[
 E(S_{\mathrm{forward}})\geq
 \left[1-\left(1-\frac{1}{n}\right)^n\right]
 E(S_{\mathrm{optimal}}).
\]
For five donors the bound is approximately 0.672. This is a worst-case
bound under the stated fixed-set assumptions, not an expected efficiency
or evidence that a particular selected set is optimal.

\subsection{Comparison with the approach presented here}

The connection to core-collection methods is the maximization of represented
allelic richness, with each fixed haplotype block treated as a multiallelic
locus. In \cde{st.detect.core}, the collection starts empty and each
selected line adds previously unrepresented block-specific haplotypes.
In \cde{st.select.enrichment}, the existing base population is retained
and candidate donors are scored for the variants they add to it. This
second task follows the same conditional-extension principle illustrated
by the nested grapevine collections of Le Cunff et al. (2008), where a
previously selected core was retained during expansion. Here the counted
alleles are complete phased marker sequences within common blocks.

Haplotype-based sequencing studies also seek complementary individuals
and reduce repeated coverage of the same variants. Bickhart et al. (2016)
examined sequencing-panel selection with haplotype-frequency weighting;
the present enrichment criterion gives each eligible additional
block-specific haplotype the same weight. Forward selection then updates
the gain against the union already represented by the base and selected
donors. The cumulative enrichment curves and the core-coverage curve
make this shared coverage principle explicit, while the fixed base
catalogue determines which variants qualify as additions.

The donor-selection procedure of Sanchez et al. (2025) likewise evaluates
candidates in relation to recipients and donors already introduced. Their
haplotypic estimated breeding values connect complementarity to predicted
performance. The criterion used here measures complementarity through
variant identity: a donor contributes when it supplies an eligible
haplotype absent from the current collection. Its output can therefore
serve as a diversity component in donor choice alongside trait-based
criteria, with the consequences for agronomic performance evaluated
separately.

The present workflow therefore combines established coverage and
complementarity principles with an explicit comparison between phased
base and donor populations, flexible common block definitions, and exact
or forward search under the same enrichment criterion. Its output
quantifies diversity available in the combined collection. It does not
predict the transmission or retention of those variants through crossing,
recombination and selection, nor does it establish an expected agronomic
gain. Those consequences require additional genetic and breeding criteria.

